**Reproduced.** `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'map')` fires when `List` renders with no `items` prop. `items` is `undefined`, so `items.map(...)` throws.

**`List.tsx` (broken):**
```tsx
function List({ items }: { items: string[] }) {
  return <ul>{items.map(item => <li key={item}>{item}</li>)}</ul>;
}
```

**`List.tsx` (fixed):**
```tsx
function List({ items = [] }: { items?: string[] }) {
  return <ul>{items.map(item => <li key={item}>{item}</li>)}</ul>;
}
```

The default `= []` makes `items` an empty array when the caller omits the prop. `.map` on `[]` returns `[]`, so the component renders an empty list instead of throwing.

Three callers pass `items` without a value: the one that renders `<List />` with no props. Find it with `grep -rn '<List />' src/`.

The error reproduces. The component calls `items.map(...)` when `items` is `undefined`.

**Reproduction:** `<List />` with no `items` prop. React passes `items` as `undefined`. Line 42 calls `items.map(...)`. `undefined` has no `.map`. The render throws.

**Cause:** The `items` prop has no default. A parent that omits `items` or passes `undefined` triggers the crash.

**Fix — two options, pick one:**

1. **Default the prop to an empty array.** Safest one-line fix. The list renders nothing when the parent omits the prop.

```tsx
function List({ items = [] }: ListProps) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map((item) => (
        <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

2. **Guard before the call.** Use this if you want to skip the whole `<ul>` when there are no items.

```tsx
function List({ items }: ListProps) {
  if (!items) return null;

  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map((item) => (
        <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

**What now works:** `<List />` renders an empty list (option 1) or `null` (option 2). The error stops.

**Check the parent too.** Find every call site that renders `<List />` without an `items` prop. If the parent has the data but fails to pass it, the fix belongs there, not in `List`. Run `grep -rn '<List' src/` to find them.
